        "original_id": "1d4633f8-6596-5edc-8cf9-8695179ab5ea",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Alberta, Canada, United Kingdom\n- Systems: Constitution of Alberta, Westminster system\n- Concepts: unwritten constitution, parliamentary procedure, laying before the house\n\nFacts:\n- The Constitution of Alberta describes the fundamental rules under which the Canadian province of Alberta is governed.\n- Alberta has an unwritten constitution, as is typical of Canadian provinces and Westminster systems.\n- The Westminster system is a parliamentary system of government modelled after the system that developed in the United Kingdom.\n- In parliamentary procedure, especially in the Westminster system, laying a document before the house means formally recognising that it has been made available for members of a deliberative assembly to read.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9bfa7891-6b2c-581f-999f-17d9c8369df4",
        "text": "Page: Constitution of Alberta. Sentence: The Constitution of Alberta describes the fundamental rules under which the Canadian province of Alberta is governed.\nPage: Constitution of Alberta. Sentence: As is typical of all Canadian provinces, and Westminster systems more generally, Alberta's is an unwritten constitution.\nPage: Westminster system. Sentence: The Westminster system is a parliamentary system of government modelled after that which developed in the United Kingdom.\nPage: Laying before the house. Sentence: In parliamentary procedure, especially the Westminster system, a document is laid before the house or Laid on the Table of the House when it is formally recognised as having been made available for members of a deliberative assembly to read.",
